WEBVTT

0
00:00.000 --> 00:01.610
<v Krista>In this video we're talking about how to</v>

1
00:01.610 --> 00:04.214
multiply and divide by powers of 10.

2
00:04.214 --> 00:06.717
When we say powers of 10, what we mean is

3
00:06.717 --> 00:08.273
10 raised to the first power,

4
00:08.273 --> 00:09.612
10 raised to the second power,

5
00:09.612 --> 00:11.900
10 raised to the third power, etcetera.

6
00:11.900 --> 00:14.370
So what we want to realize is that 10 to the first

7
00:14.370 --> 00:16.041
is just going to be equal to 10.

8
00:16.041 --> 00:19.650
That 10 squared is going to be equal to 100.

9
00:19.650 --> 00:23.500
That 10 cubed is going to be equal to 1000.

10
00:23.500 --> 00:24.864
What you'll notice is that when we have

11
00:24.864 --> 00:27.079
10 to the first, obviously we know that's equal to 10.

12
00:27.079 --> 00:29.009
When we add another power of 10,

13
00:29.009 --> 00:31.588
we just take the decimal place that was here

14
00:31.588 --> 00:33.106
and move it one spot to the right

15
00:33.106 --> 00:34.928
to get from 10 to the first equals 10

16
00:34.928 --> 00:37.243
to 10 to the second equals 100.

17
00:37.243 --> 00:39.488
Then 10 cubed we're adding another power of 10

18
00:39.488 --> 00:42.059
so instead of 100, we have the decimal place right here,

19
00:42.059 --> 00:43.880
we move the decimal place one more

20
00:43.880 --> 00:45.554
spot to the right to get 1000.

21
00:45.554 --> 00:47.597
So in other words, what we know right off the bat

22
00:47.597 --> 00:49.659
is that every time we add a power of 10,

23
00:49.659 --> 00:52.025
when we go from 10 to the first to 10 to the second

24
00:52.025 --> 00:54.088
and then from 10 to the second to 10 to the third,

25
00:54.088 --> 00:56.335
we just move the decimal place one spot to the right,

26
00:56.335 --> 00:58.442
or basically just add a zero.

27
00:58.442 --> 01:00.018
So let's do some examples here.

28
01:00.018 --> 01:02.409
We have 42 times 10.

29
01:02.409 --> 01:06.235
Well, 42, this is going to be 42.0 basically.

30
01:06.235 --> 01:08.728
We're going to move the decimal place one spot to the right

31
01:08.728 --> 01:11.103
and fill in a zero and the result here

32
01:11.103 --> 01:13.695
is just going to be 420.

33
01:13.695 --> 01:15.925
Basically because we multiplied by 10,

34
01:15.925 --> 01:17.792
we just added the zero right here.

35
01:17.792 --> 01:21.244
Same thing here. We have 625 times 1000.

36
01:21.244 --> 01:22.924
Well we just count the number of zeroes

37
01:22.924 --> 01:24.609
that we have in our power of 10.

38
01:24.609 --> 01:26.782
With three zeroes in 1000 which means

39
01:26.782 --> 01:29.161
we're going to add three zeroes to 625.

40
01:29.161 --> 01:33.674
So we get 625 and then add one, two, three zeroes

41
01:33.674 --> 01:35.624
which of course you can see is the same thing as

42
01:35.624 --> 01:38.280
saying we had our decimal place here after 625

43
01:38.280 --> 01:41.142
and we moved it one, two, three spots to the right

44
01:41.142 --> 01:43.750
because there were 3 zeroes and then we just filled those

45
01:43.750 --> 01:48.331
spots in with zeroes to get a final answer of 625,000.

46
01:48.331 --> 01:50.707
Now division by powers of 10 is going to be exactly

47
01:50.707 --> 01:53.291
the same thing except instead of moving the decimal place

48
01:53.291 --> 01:55.490
to the right and filling in with zeros,

49
01:55.490 --> 01:56.850
we're going to move the decimal place

50
01:56.850 --> 01:58.480
to the left and fill in with zeroes.

51
01:58.480 --> 02:03.063
So here we have 53.0, right, and if we move the decimal

52
02:03.063 --> 02:06.196
to the left two places because we have two zeroes here

53
02:06.196 --> 02:08.756
in our power of 10, we get one, two,

54
02:08.756 --> 02:10.839
which is the same as .53.

55
02:12.965 --> 02:15.161
In other words because we had one, two, zeros,

56
02:15.161 --> 02:17.632
we moved our decimal place to the left two spots

57
02:17.632 --> 02:20.262
because we were dividing. So we got point 53.

58
02:20.262 --> 02:23.669
If we have 170 divided by 1000,

59
02:23.669 --> 02:25.902
we have three zeros here in our power of 10,

60
02:25.902 --> 02:27.967
one, two, three, which means we want to move

61
02:27.967 --> 02:30.400
our decimal to the left, three places.

62
02:30.400 --> 02:34.400
So one, two, three and we get an answer of .170.

63
02:35.980 --> 02:38.831
Of course we can simplify that to .17

64
02:38.831 --> 02:40.495
by dropping the trailing zero.

65
02:40.495 --> 02:42.192
You can also simply this problem

66
02:42.192 --> 02:43.648
before you do your division.

67
02:43.648 --> 02:45.236
When you have a number here that ends in zero

68
02:45.236 --> 02:47.480
and you're dividing by a power of 10,

69
02:47.480 --> 02:49.274
you can cancel a zero from each number

70
02:49.274 --> 02:50.525
before you do the division.

71
02:50.525 --> 02:52.593
So in other words, we could have canceled the

72
02:52.593 --> 02:55.986
zero here and a corresponding zero here

73
02:55.986 --> 02:59.819
and then the problem becomes 17 divided by 100

74
03:00.794 --> 03:02.310
and you have two zeroes so you would move

75
03:02.310 --> 03:03.935
the decimal place one, two spots

76
03:03.935 --> 03:06.268
to the left and you get .17.

77
03:06.268 --> 03:08.090
So in other words we can get rid of the trailing

78
03:08.090 --> 03:10.949
zero here in our original problem and then do the division

79
03:10.949 --> 03:12.438
or do the division first and then

80
03:12.438 --> 03:15.548
get rid of that trailing zero here to get our final answer.

81
03:15.548 --> 03:17.243
Now we can also do this with decimals, right?

82
03:17.243 --> 03:18.933
All of these look like they were whole numbers.

83
03:18.933 --> 03:21.183
We had 42, 625, 53 and 170.

84
03:23.386 --> 03:25.806
But those were all just whole numbers with a decimal was

85
03:25.806 --> 03:27.064
to the right of the number.

86
03:27.064 --> 03:30.760
Here we have 2.5 times 100 and the decimal is right here

87
03:30.760 --> 03:31.959
in the middle of our number.

88
03:31.959 --> 03:34.674
Well, because we're multiplying by 100 and we have

89
03:34.674 --> 03:36.580
one, two, zeroes, we're still just going to move

90
03:36.580 --> 03:38.596
our decimal two places to the right.

91
03:38.596 --> 03:42.064
So one, two, and then we want to fill in with zeros.

92
03:42.064 --> 03:44.772
So we get an answer of 250.

93
03:44.772 --> 03:46.915
So we just counted the zeros, we had two of them,

94
03:46.915 --> 03:48.737
we moved our decimal place two to the right

95
03:48.737 --> 03:50.780
because we were multiplying and we filled in

96
03:50.780 --> 03:52.861
with the trailing zero to get 250.

97
03:52.861 --> 03:55.114
We can also divide a decimal by a power of 10.

98
03:55.114 --> 03:56.414
Here because we're dividing,

99
03:56.414 --> 03:58.504
we know we're moving our decimal to the left

100
03:58.504 --> 04:00.728
as opposed to moving it to the right when we multiply.

101
04:00.728 --> 04:05.201
We have one, two, three, four, zeros in our power of 10

102
04:05.201 --> 04:08.375
so we're going to go one, two, three, four,

103
04:08.375 --> 04:11.203
just make these little buckets for place holder zeros

104
04:11.203 --> 04:14.034
and then fill them in one, two, three.

105
04:14.034 --> 04:18.117
So then our final answer is going to be .0006282.

106
04:20.420 --> 04:22.482
So just remember when you're multiplying,

107
04:22.482 --> 04:24.339
you move your decimal point to the right.

108
04:24.339 --> 04:26.200
When you're dividing, you move your

109
04:26.200 --> 04:27.578
decimal point to the left.

110
04:27.578 --> 04:30.989
All you do is count the number of zeros in your power of 10,

111
04:30.989 --> 04:33.626
move the decimal point either to the right or to the left,

112
04:33.626 --> 04:34.841
the matching amount of places,

113
04:34.841 --> 04:36.308
and then fill in those places,

114
04:36.308 --> 04:40.141
either on the right or on the left with zeros.